"Microsoft Frontier Company: AI engineering that amplifies and protects your intelligence Microsoft launched 'Microsoft Frontier Company', a dedicated organization to support customers' AI infrastructure construction and business performance creation and protect intellectual property rights. Microsoft established the 'Microsoft Frontier Company' to help companies achieve practical results through AI investments and accumulate their own data and knowledge. To achieve this, we plan to invest $2.5 billion and deploy 6,000 industry and engineering experts directly to customers to jointly develop and improve customized AI systems. We are collaborating with global companies such as LSEG (London Stock Exchange Group), Unilever, and Novo Nordisk, and are also strengthening FDE cooperation with global consulting partners. Bringing more control over your connectors Mistral AI has introduced new administrator controls and security features for connectors to enhance secure integration with external enterprise platforms. Provides enhanced administrator controls to manage connector access and control individual tools at the workspace and organization level Connector-scoped API keys and multi-account connector support to prevent impersonation when integrating third-party systems in automated AI operations Provides a connector debugger and Vibe Code/Workflows integration for detailed root cause analysis of non-functioning connections Workflows for work that runs the business Mistral AI has released 'Workflows', an orchestration layer that supports stable transfer of enterprise AI processes from proof-of-concept to production, as a public preview. Workflows is the orchestration layer of enterprise AI, providing process durability, observability, and fault tolerance. Once developers write a workflow in Python, they can publish it to Le Chat so anyone in the organization can run it, and every step is tracked and audited in Studio. Automates global shipping document review and KYC processes by providing human approval steps (wait_for_input) that can be paused and resumed and detailed execution history recording. Introducing Forge Mistral AI has launched 'Forge', a system that helps companies build customized artificial intelligence models and agents by learning from their own data, code base, and expertise. Unlike general-purpose AI that focuses on open data, it operates customized to the company by learning internal company regulations, source code, and documents. It supports training methods throughout the model life cycle, including pre-training, post-training, and reinforcement learning. By allowing companies to retain control over their proprietary data and models, it ensures security and strategic autonomy within a regulated environment. Mistral AI partners with NVIDIA to accelerate open frontier models Mistral AI and NVIDIA are collaborating as founding members in the 'NVIDIA Nemotron Alliance', a global initiative to accelerate the development of open, cutting-edge foundation models. We plan to jointly develop cutting-edge open source AI models by combining Mistral AI's professional model architecture with NVIDIA's computing resources and model development tools. The Nemotron Alliance's first initiative is a base model that will be trained on the NVIDIA DGX Cloud and serve as the basis for the upcoming NVIDIA Nemotron 4 product family. Mistral AI releases Mistral Small 4 to strengthen its open model ecosystem and support developer innovation. Leanstral: Open-Source foundation for trustworthy vibe-coding Mistral AI has launched Leanstral, the first open source coding agent specializing in formal verification and software specification verification, supporting Lean 4 proof assistants. To solve the human manual verification bottleneck, which is a limitation of AI coding agents, we developed Leanstral, which performs formal mathematical proofs at the same time as development. It is based on a highly efficient, sparse architecture with 6B active parameters, has weights released under the Apache 2.0 license, and supports a free API. Introduced the benchmark suite FLTEval to evaluate performance in an actual structured repository environment beyond competition math problems. Joyride Through July With 12 Games Coming to GeForce NOW NVIDIA is adding 12 new games to its GeForce NOW cloud gaming service in July and holding a summer sale event. Monopoly: Star Wars Heroes vs. A total of 12 new games, including Villains, will be added to the GeForce NOW Cloud in July. You can play games via cloud streaming on almost any device: mobile, PC, Mac, TV, tablet, and handheld device. The GeForce NOW summer sale is coming to an end, including $35 off a 12-month Performance membership and $70 off an Ultimate membership. NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout NVIDIA has introduced a new computing support business model based on revenue sharing and credit support to help startups and companies build AI infrastructure. As AI computational demands move from model development to the production inference phase that generates large-scale tokens, we are introducing new business models to increase access to large-scale accelerated computing. NVIDIA provides infrastructure to AI cloud partners and cooperates by sharing a portion of cloud sales based on operating capacity in addition to standard product sales. Together with partners such as Sharon AI and Firmus, we have begun expanding our global infrastructure, including deploying Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs and building a large-scale AI data center campus. Z.ai launches ZCode to challenge Cursor, Claude Code and GitHub Copilot in AI coding Z.ai has officially launched ZCode, a free, agent-based AI coding desktop application optimized for its GLM-5.2 large-scale language model. Provides an agent-based development environment that thinks in project units optimized for the GLM-5.2 model Monitor and control coding agents running in real time through mobile messengers such as WeChat, Feishu, Telegram, etc. Supports integration with third-party models (Claude Code, Gemini, etc.) and offers cheaper rates compared to other companies’ services NVIDIA BioNeMo accelerates Anthropic Claude Science Claude Science, Antropic's research AI workbench, has been integrated with NVIDIA's BioNeMo agent toolkit to accelerate computational life science research. Antropic has launched the public beta of ‘Clode Science’, which allows scientists to run end-to-end research workflows by conversing with digital agents in natural language. This integration provides NVIDIA Bionemo's accelerated models, computational libraries, and NVIDIA NIM microservices as callable skills within the cloud science environment. Scientists can instruct tasks such as genome sequence analysis, protein structure prediction, and molecular binder design using natural language and receive accelerated results without having to manually configure complex software environments. Microsoft launches a $2.5 billion AI deployment business with 6,000 engineers Microsoft has launched a new division, 'Microsoft Frontier', with an investment of $2.5 billion and 6,000 engineers to help companies apply AI in practice. Microsoft unveiled Microsoft Frontier, a 6,000 expert and $2.5 billion investment to accelerate enterprise AI deployment. Big tech companies are moving beyond simply selling AI software to a competition where they directly deploy engineers to customer sites to prove actual business performance. Major AI companies such as AWS, OpenAI, and Anthropic are also launching similar AI field deployment engineering ventures worth hundreds of millions to billions of dollars one after another. Tesla delivered 480,126 vehicles in Q2, far exceeding the 406,000 Wall Street expected Tesla succeeded in rebounding its performance by delivering 480,126 vehicles in the second quarter of 2026, significantly exceeding market expectations. Tesla's second-quarter deliveries of 480,126 units exceeded Wall Street expectations of 406,000 units by 18%, up 25% year-over-year and up 34% quarter-over-quarter. Factors cited for the performance rebound include the launch of the low-priced Model 3 and Model Y, the launch of FSD (supervisory type) in Europe, and the rise in European oil prices due to the war with Iran. Tesla halted production of the Model S and Why the next leap in AI video is teaching avatars to see and listen Artificial intelligence video technology is transitioning from a competition to simply improve image quality to a competition to develop avatar models that interact with users in real time. The AI ​​avatar's interaction abilities are categorized into three levels: speaking (level 1), listening and reacting (level 2), and looking, listening and speaking (level 3). Level 2 is a key breakthrough that recognizes the user's voice in real time and provides natural feedback such as a head nod or short answer. Reducing the latency of video models to less than one second to enable natural, real-time conversations is becoming a new technological barrier. Getty scraps its $3.7bn Shutterstock merger after a UK regulator won’t budge The $3.7 billion merger deal between Getty Images and Shutterstock has collapsed following the UK Competition Authority's (CMA) demand for sale terms. The US Department of Justice unconditionally approved the merger, but the UK Competition and Markets Authority (CMA) set the sale of Shutterstock's global editorial division as a condition of approval, and the merger was thwarted. The two companies pursued a merger to reduce costs and respond to the shrinking market caused by AI image generators, but the board of directors unanimously decided to terminate the contract on July 6. The collapse of this merger once again demonstrates the UK's CMA's stronger regulatory influence on global big tech and media transactions. A cheap Chinese AI model is closing in on Anthropic and OpenAI Chinese startup Z.ai has launched GLM-5.2, an open-weight AI model that approaches the performance of U.S. OpenAI and Anthropic at a low cost. The GLM-5.2 model ranked 4th in Artificial Analysis' Intelligence Index v4.1, ranking first among open weight models. The model was trained and run on a cluster of 100,000 Chinese Huawei Ascend 910B processors without any U.S. semiconductor equipment. It is released under the MIT license and can be downloaded and modified for free, and the API usage price is very cheap, at one-fifth to one-seventh of the US competitive model. Microsoft launches its own AI deployment company with $2.5 billion commitment Microsoft has decided to establish ‘Microsoft Frontier Company’ and invest $2.5 billion to support the successful adoption of AI by companies. Microsoft announced the establishment of ‘Microsoft Frontier Company’, which is dedicated to deploying enterprise AI using existing AI tools. The venture will involve an investment of $2.5 billion and 6,000 industrial and engineering experts. Major tech companies such as Amazon (AWS), OpenAI, and Antropic have recently launched similar AI field deployment engineering (FDE) projects, intensifying competition. Yep, we’re using OpenClaw to date now This is a story about how users are using OpenClaw, an open source AI agent, to innovate and simplify the dating process, such as automating Instagram reel uploads or recommending date spots. Content creator and startup founder Ben Guez used OpenClaw and Claude to automatically upload Instagram Reels targeting women from specific countries based on World Cup results, receiving hundreds of DMs. Guez also uses it for app marketing by encouraging users to download Canary, an AI language learning app he developed, in order to receive DM replies. Tech PR firm founder Jeff Weisbein uses OpenClaw to research dating destinations and places to eat in various parts of South Florida and organize them into linked documents. Indian tech tycoon bets $30M of his own money to build AI alternative to Microsoft Office It is reported that Indian serial entrepreneur Babin Turakia invested $30 million of his personal funds to build ‘Neo’, an AI-based collaboration platform corresponding to Microsoft Office. Indian entrepreneur Babin Turakia independently invested (bootstrapping) $30 million of his personal funds in the development of 'Neo', a business platform redesigned from the ground up for the AI ​​era. Neo integrates project management, documents, file storage, and AI into a single product, and has an independent design that is not dependent on a specific artificial intelligence model. We plan to release the software within a few months, targeting knowledge workers in small and medium-sized businesses and professional service industries such as technology and consulting first. OpenAI floats giving Trump administration 5 percent cut of AI boom OpenAI has proposed offering a 5% stake to the U.S. government in a bid to ease tensions with the Trump administration and reduce public backlash. OpenAI CEO Sam Altman has proposed government ownership as the best way for the public to share in the benefits of AI, and he first pitched the idea to Trump early last year. Based on OpenAI's most recent valuation of $852 billion, a 5% stake would be worth about $42.6 billion, and the proposal includes other U.S. AI companies offering similar stakes to the government. The U.S. government is taking a very interventionist approach to the AI ​​industry under the Trump administration, having already acquired a 10% stake in Intel and demanding 15% of AI chip sales from China from Nvidia and AMD. 10 Agentic AI Frameworks You Should Know in 2026 We introduce and compare the key agent creation AI frameworks you need to know in 2026. LangGraph provides granular control over complex, long-running workflows by modeling states and transitions as a graph. CrewAI is great for quickly prototyping role-based multi-agent systems. Agent frameworks have evolved beyond simple LLM wrappers to provide state, memory, tooling, evaluation, and deployment management capabilities. Humanity’s Last Exam is a Distraction It covers the development background and various expert opinions of 'Humanity's Last Exam (HLE)', an extremely difficult benchmark for measuring the reasoning and deep knowledge abilities of AI systems. As traditional AI evaluation methods become ineffective, the Center for AI Safety and Scale AI collaborated with world-class experts to develop an extremely challenging HLE benchmark. HLE consists of more than 2,500 expert-level questions in more than 100 academic fields that require complex deductive reasoning and deep understanding rather than simple memorization or multiple choice questions. Even state-of-the-art frontier models such as GPT, Gemini, and Claude show low accuracy rates of 45-50% on this test and tend to be overly confident in incorrect answers. The MMM Data Model -- A Normative Specification for Knowledge Interoperability in a Decentralisable Knowledge Commons We propose MMM, a new knowledge data model that overcomes the limitations of document-centric knowledge information systems and supports interoperability between various disciplines and applications. Existing document-centered knowledge structuring methods limit the updating, sharing, and reuse of knowledge, and standardized approaches do not prioritize human usability, making them difficult to popularize. The MMM data model is designed to be interoperable without semantic convergence by combining the expressive freedom of free text labels with a small number of normative constraints. Reference implementation and pilot deployment data validated the model's implementability and initial usability. Making Failure Safe: A Constrained, Verifiable Agent Framework for Open-Web Data Collection This study proposes a constraint-based agent framework that converts LLM output into typed JSON collector configurations instead of free-form code to perform reliable and verifiable web scraping from natural language requirements. Direct web scraper creation through LLM is unstable due to dependency errors, broken selectors, schema inconsistencies, etc. We propose a framework that combines six types of collector classification system, template/utility function constraints, static Airflow DAG execution, rule-based quality checking, and feedback correction mechanism. Experiments on 80 independently source-verified tasks show the lowest average execution time without consuming any LLM tokens during the execution phase. Solution space path planning for supporting en-route air traffic control This study developed and evaluated an interpretable and computationally efficient Solvable Spatial Path Planning (SSPP) algorithm taking into account the needs and operational constraints of air traffic controllers. To overcome the limitations of existing route planning algorithms, we designed an algorithm that combines the decision-making logic of air traffic controllers with the flexibility of the solution space display. We integrated three intent-based conflict detection methods into a resolution space framework: distance-based, time interval-based, and zone-based. Vertex-based (SSPPV) and edge-based (SSPPE) search nodes were proposed, and experimental results showed that the combination of SSPPV and zone-based collision detection performed best. RareDxR1: Autonomous Medical Reasoning for Rare Disease Diagnosis Beyond Human Annotation This study proposes RareDxR1, a medical macrolanguage model with autonomous and progressive learning and reasoning capabilities to diagnose rare diseases directly from unstructured clinical records. To solve the bottleneck and information loss problems of structured phenotype extraction or retrieval augmented generation (RAG), we developed RareDxR1, an end-to-end inference-driven LLM. We proposed a Reflection-Enhanced Reasoning Sampling (RERS) technique that directly internalizes fragmented rare disease knowledge into model parameters and synthesizes expert-level diagnostic pathways by learning from failures without human annotation. The dual-level curriculum is designed to gradually master rare disease diagnosis through reinforcement learning, and has achieved the highest level of accuracy compared to existing techniques in benchmark tests. From Signals to Structure: How Memory Architecture Drives Language Emergence in LLM Agents This study shows that memory architecture plays a more decisive role in stably coordinating cooperative language codes than channel capacity in the language expression process between LLM agents. Agents with a persistent private laptop memory architecture utilized surplus channel capacity to achieve the most reliable language coordination. Stateless agents showed optimal performance with moderate channel capacity, but performance degraded when the vocabulary exceeded the processing limits of the rolling context window. Contrary to theoretical predictions based on information bottlenecks, in reality, securing excess capacity is more advantageous for forming stable language conventions. Representation as a Bottleneck for Mechanistic Interpretability: The Manifestation Unit Protocol In order to increase the reusability of existing analysis results in a mechanistic interpretability study that analyzes neural network components, this study proposed a structured tuple protocol called 'Manifestation Unit' and verified its effectiveness. A structured expression layer was introduced to solve the problem of existing mechanistic interpretability research results being trapped in laptop files, making reuse and linkage to sub-tasks difficult. For the transformer architecture, we proposed Manifestation Units, a structured tuple protocol (E, S, R, D, G) that extends the attention head primitive type. By applying this protocol to GPT-2, CNN, and beta-VAE, we confirmed that search performance was improved, the causal sufficiency and necessity of CNN filters were satisfied, and circuit members were restored. SNAP-FM: Sparse Nonlinear Accelerated Projection for Physics-Constrained Generative Modeling We propose a method to accelerate the nonlinear constrained projection problem, which is a bottleneck in physically constrained generative modeling, through sparse nonlinear optimization techniques. Constraint-based sampling, which enforces physical constraints such as conservation laws and boundary conditions on the output of the generative model, incurs very high computational costs during inference. We presented a solution by leveraging sample-wise batch and local PDE combination structures (block-sparse Jacobian and KKT systems) and combining GPU sparse factorization with ExaModels.jl and MadNLP.jl. By applying physical constraint flow matching (PCFM), we accelerated nonlinear projection speed while maintaining constraint satisfaction in PDE benchmarks with one-dimensional and two-dimensional linear and nonlinear constraints. GRPO, Dr. GRPO, and DAPO Are Three Operations on One Number: The Group-Standard-Deviation Identity This study proved that the three methods (GRPO, Dr. GRPO, DAPO) widely used for inference learning of language models are ultimately based on the same principle of adjusting the group standard deviation, which is a single number that represents the degree of disagreement between sampled answers for each prompt. GRPO, Dr. GRPO and DAPO are not separate techniques, but merely different settings of one dial called the group standard deviation. In a correct/incorrect reward system, the degree of disagreement (standard deviation) between answers exactly matches the size of the learning update, and groups that are in perfect agreement contribute nothing to learning. The results of this study help us determine which problems should be given more weight and how many attempts each problem is required, which we validated using the Big-Math dataset and controlled learning experiments. EVOTS: Evolutionary Transformer Search for Time Series Forecasting We propose EVOTS, an evolutionary neural architecture exploration framework to explore and design task-adaptive transformer architectures for multivariate time series prediction. Using modular genomic representation, we introduce a flexible combination of attention, feedforward, and projection components and a repair mechanism that maintains structural validity. Performance was evaluated on the ETT benchmark datasets (ETTh1, ETTh2, ETTm1, ETTm2) and various forecasting settings (univariate and multivariate, 96–720 periods). We achieved competitive and improved mean square error (MSE) compared to robust transformer-based baseline models in multivariate-to-multivariate prediction settings. FRAME: Learning the Adaptation Domain with a Mixture of Fractional-Fourier Experts This study proposes FRAME, a Fractional-Fourier Expert MoE adapter that continuously learns the optimal frequency domain (between space and Fourier domain) in which the adapter will operate in Parameter Efficient Refinement (PEFT). To overcome the limitations of existing LoRA (spatial domain) and spectral adapter (Fourier domain), each expert has a learnable fractional Fourier order to find the optimal domain within the spatial-spectral continuum on its own. The mutual inconsistency of fractional Fourier operators of different orders allows natural decorrelation between experts, reducing interference and improving multi-tasking capabilities. In common sense, math, code, and knowledge benchmark tests using the LLaMA-3.1-8B and Qwen2.5-7B models, it showed better performance than spectral reference models such as existing MoE-LoRA, FlyLoRA, FourierMoE, and HMoRA. Persona Without Substrate: Regime-Dependence and the LLM Individuation Problem We question the assumption that the existing ontological framework for the LLM individuation problem assumes that the same direction represents the same content across manipulations during prompt conditioning, fine-tuning, and inference, and propose experimental evidence to refute this and a new alternative framework. The existing LLM individualization framework of Beckmann & Butlin (2026) is premised on the assumption that the same vector direction means the same content under multiple conditions (prompt, fine-tuning, manipulation during inference), but this has not been verified. Through persona topology experiments targeting the Qwen3-4B-Instruct and Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 models, we presented four experimental evidences that refute this assumption. To overcome these limitations, we propose a 'regime indexed individuation' framework in which the identity unit of representational content must be a (means, regime) pair rather than a means alone. Controllable Narrative Rendering for Enhanced Assisted Writing A study of the Loom framework proposed to resolve the dilemma between narrative fidelity and description strength that large language models (LLMs) face when supporting creative writing. Existing LLM writing support has a dichotomous limit that oscillates between simple correction and indiscriminate plot expansion. The proposed Loom framework precisely controls the density of narrative intent and description based on the narratological distinction of story and discourse. Loom uses a three-stage pipeline that separates syntactic embedding from epistemic material generation to enhance writing without violating the original event structure. Harnessing the Latent Space: From Steering Vectors to Model Calibrators for Control and Trust This is a paper on how to control the model's output and evaluate its reliability by utilizing the internal representation (latent space) of a large language model. As language models grow in size, it becomes increasingly difficult to understand and control their internal representation. We propose steering vectors in latent space to control the model. To ensure reliability, we develop latent space-based model calibrators to provide insights utilizing internal states. Benchmarking Frontier LLMs on Arabic Cultural and Sociolinguistic Knowledge: A Cross-Evaluation Framework with Human SME Ground Truth This study proposes a peer-evaluation framework using ground truth data from human experts to benchmark and evaluate frontier LLMs on cultural and sociolinguistic knowledge of Arabic. We constructed 103 validated prompt-rubric pairs written and graded by native speaking experts targeting the Egyptian and Iraqi Arabic dialect communities. Among the automatic evaluation models (judge models), GPT-5.4 was found to be the most reliable judge, but most judge models showed systematically lenient scoring tendencies. In all judge models, implicit cultural inference, which requires imitation of native speaker judgment rather than lexical verification, was revealed to be the main failure factor in automatic evaluation. Readable but Not Controllable: Neuron-Level Evidence for Medical LLM Hallucination Research results show that hallucinations in large language models (LLMs) in the medical field can be easily detected through internal activation analysis, but correction through control of related neurons is difficult. Using simple, well-conditioned probes, hallucinations in medical LLMs can be reliably detected with high AUROC scores ranging from 0.77 to 0.86. Hallucinatory signals are not localized to specific regions but exist randomly and redundantly distributed across hundreds of neurons. Although it is easy to detect the internal structures responsible for hallucinations, the large gap between detection and control abilities makes it difficult to correct hallucinations by directly stimulating the relevant neurons. When re-verifying the paper “Frontier AI beats medical professional tools,” the agreement between raters was 0.10, and raters were participants. A re-verification of the Nature Medicine paper, which claimed that general-purpose frontier AI was superior to medical AI tools, revealed serious evaluation limitations, such as very low agreement between raters and the fact that the raters were participants. The paper ‘General-purpose large language models outperform specialized clinical AI tools on medical benchmarks’ published in Nature Medicine has been re-verified. As a result of re-verification, the inter-rater agreement between the raters who participated in the evaluation was found to be extremely low at 0.10. Issues of conflict of interest and loss of objectivity were pointed out in that the rater who evaluated the medical AI tool was also a participant in the experiment. Show GN: Page distribution platform for internal users This is an introduction to the internal distribution platform developed to easily distribute and share documents such as HTML, Markdown, and OpenAPI specifications that are accessible only to internal users. Easily distribute back-office API documents or internal data analysis reports that require security through links that can only be read by in-house workspace users. It supports HTML, Markdown, and OpenAPI specification (redoc) formats, and provides a simple CLI that allows CI or AI to automatically update the page. We include affordable paid plans to cover server operating and maintenance costs. Box3D - Open source 3D physics engine released Box3D, an open source 3D physics engine developed by extending the design of Box2D to control large-scale 3D physics simulations on game servers, has been released. We began development ourselves to overcome the limitations of Chaos, the basic physics engine of Unreal Engine (not supporting gyroscopic torque, errors in simulation of falling trees, etc.) We started by forking Rubikon-Lite and replaced most of the code to make it compatible with the Box2D architecture while reflecting the optimization techniques of Box2D v3.0. Provides features specialized for large-scale server-side 3D physics control, including C17 source, C API, substepping solver, cross-platform determinism, and double-based large world support. Fable 5 is back The return of Claude Fable 5, which updated its cybersecurity safeguards after Anthropic's talks with the U.S. government, sparks community controversy over its security and possible leaks. Anthropic has updated Fable 5's cybersecurity guardrails following U.S. government consultation, and even benign requests may result in short-term false positives and a fallback to Opus 4.8. Paid plan users can use Fable 5 up to 50% of their weekly limit until July 7, after which they will need to use a different model or credits. The information technology community points out the risk of leakage of model weights, and there are conflicting opinions: Anthropic's risk warning is a marketing exaggeration, and technical speculation that it is encrypted and protected with Nvidia TEE (Trusted Execution Environment), etc. Uruky: One-month trial of paid European search engine This article is about my experience using Europe-based paid search engine Uruky for a month and analyzing its pros and cons compared to Kagi. Uruky emphasizes privacy and digital sovereignty through European hosting, dedicated search, use of European search providers, and a very easy sign-up process. It offers detailed search customization, has a reasonable price of 5 euros per month, and has a good interface, but it still lacks the key quality of search results. Even though it used EUSP, a European search index, it showed unexpected results of lower quality than Ecosia, and it is still considered unsatisfactory to be used as an everyday search engine. [D] Self-Promotion Thread This is to encourage participation by creating an experimental self-promotion thread in Reddit's MachineLearning subreddit where you can promote personal projects, startups, products, etc. You can post personal projects, startups, product promotions, collaboration requests, blogs, and more. Payment and pricing information for products and services must be mentioned, and shortened links or automatic subscription links are prohibited. This is an experimental thread that may be canceled based on community feedback, and is designed to reduce spam on the main thread. What do you think about paper fishing? [D] This is a post asking about the opinion of a doctoral student at a research group in Germany about the practice of 'paper fishing', which involves posting one's name on other people's papers without actually contributing to the research, and this academic practice. Within a research group in Germany, there was a case of a doctoral student who did no research at all and only listed his name as a co-author on another's paper for the purpose of receiving research funds. This free-riding behavior is being abused as a way for professors to prove their performance and renew their funding. Although the author feels that this behavior is unethical, he has received feedback that so-called 'paper phishing', such as professors posting the names of acquaintances, is common in academia. IN 2026 ML BOOK OUTDATED? [D] This is a Reddit question asking whether the book 'Hands-on Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras, and TensorFlow (3rd edition)' will still be useful for machine learning learning in 2026 or if it will be outdated. Learning validity questions about the 1200-page 3rd edition book covering Scikit-Learn, Keras, and TensorFlow. Inquire whether the book will be outdated for introduction to machine learning by 2026 Ask if there is a better machine learning learning alternative other than this book. The Control Plane Was the Point: Revisiting autofz in the LLM Era In the LLM era, the importance of the control plane in how to efficiently distribute fixed resources among multiple imperfect workers is reexamined through the example of meta fuzzer autofz. There is no single fuzzer or tool that is always superior in all situations, and performance varies greatly depending on the target system. A rank inversion phenomenon occurs in which the most effective tool continues to change during the testing process. Distributing resources equally across all tools is wasteful, and resources must be distributed efficiently through feedback and dynamic decisions. Editorial: It's time to step up and have your say for science The U.S. Office of Management and Budget (OMB) has proposed new rules aimed at interfering with political priorities and excluding the input of scientific experts from scientific research funding decisions. If OMB's proposed rule is finalized, research funding could be revoked depending on political whim. New bureaucratic barriers may arise that impede basic scientific activities such as presenting papers or attending academic conferences. Unlike executive orders, these rules are legally binding, making them much more difficult to challenge in court. Plug-in solar poses 6 safety risks, say electrical industry groups - here's when to call a pro Electrical industry groups are urging caution with plug-in solar installation kits, warning of six major safety risks. Several UK electricity industry groups have issued a joint warning about the safety of plug-in solar kits. Bi-directional power flow can degrade the functionality of ground fault circuit interrupters (RCDs/GFCIs) and can place additional stress on aging home wiring, resulting in localized cable overheating and fire hazards. Installation risks were pointed out, such as the influx of low-priced products into the market with uneven quality and unclear compliance with regulations, and abnormal cable passage methods. Finally, I found a robot vacuum that leaves my two-year-old model in the dust This is a review of the Eufy Omni S2, a robot vacuum cleaner that improves the shortcomings of the existing S1 model and shows excellent performance. Omni S2 solves the detergent container expansion problem of the existing S1 model by adding a top vent. It has a highly efficient mop roller that cleans your floors with clean water and a vacuum that excels at cleaning up pet hair. A new scent diffuser has been added to reduce odors, but the water tank needs to be emptied and filled more frequently and the battery life is a bit short at 150 minutes. I tested the LG C6 OLED for a week, and the colors made me question my Mini LED loyalty ZDNET's LG C6 OLED TV product review and experience The LG C6 OLED TV offers a brighter screen than the previous model and shows excellent performance in terms of picture quality and color accuracy. This product has a thin screen design, a dedicated screen mode for live sports and games, Dolby Vision HDR, and Dolby Atmos virtual surround sound. The newly designed processor utilizes machine learning algorithms to support automatic picture quality and sound processing tailored to the space. How I set OpenAI API usage limits to stop agent overspending and other AI billing nightmares Explains how to set usage limits and warnings and their importance when using the OpenAI API to prevent excessive charges due to agent malfunctions, etc. OpenAI API is divided into payment levels (Tier 1 to 5) depending on the user's accumulated spending amount, and the maximum monthly usage limit is automatically set for each level. If your accumulated spending exceeds $1,000 and you move up to a higher tier, it is risky as you can spend up to $200,000 per month. To prevent this, users must set their own API Spending Limits and Spending Alerts. ToddyCat-Linked Umbrij Malware Abuses OAuth to Access Gmail via Google API The ToddyCat threat group is developing and distributing Umbrij, a new malware that exploits OAuth to gain access to Gmail through Google APIs. Attackers use the Shadow Token via Remote Debug (STRD) technique to take over an active Gmail session in a Chromium-based browser via the remote debugging port and headless mode. It exploits legitimate digital signature files (binaries related to Bitdefender, MS Visual Studio, and Google Desktop) to execute Umbrij via DLL side-loading. Based on .NET obfuscated with ConfuserEx, Umbrij includes targeting specific browsers, the ability to save user profile screenshots as PDFs, and more. FortiBleed Credential Theft Linked to INC and Lynx Ransomware Operations It has been revealed that FortiBleed, a FortiGate credential theft campaign, is directly linked to the INC and Lynx ransomware operations. For the first time, a direct link between large-scale FortiGate credential theft and ransomware deployment has been confirmed, with operators connected to the FortiBleed infrastructure actively working on the INC and Lynx ransomware negotiation panels. The campaign targeted 430,000 FortiGate firewalls worldwide and harvested more than 110 million credentials, with at least 12 ransomware deployments using the collected credentials. Based on tools, logs, and operating hours, this activity appears to be the work of a Russian-speaking Initial Infiltration Broker (IAB), an organized operation comprised of approximately 20 people. SharePoint RCE CVE-2026-45659 Added to CISA KEV After Active Exploitation The U.S. CISA added a remote code execution vulnerability (CVE-2026-45659) in the Microsoft SharePoint server, for which active exploitation was confirmed, to the KEV catalog and ordered federal agencies to patch it. CISA has registered CVE-2026-45659, a high-risk RCE vulnerability caused by deserialization of untrusted data, to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog. The vulnerability could allow an authenticated attacker with at least site member privileges to remotely execute code without administrator privileges. During a recent ransomware investigation, it was confirmed that two different attackers (Storm-2603, etc.) simultaneously infiltrated the same network, gaining permanent access and bypassing security detection. NVIDIA unveils 'Aspire', a robot AI that writes and debugs control code on its own NVIDIA has unveiled 'Aspire', a robot learning system that improves performance by writing control code and debugging errors. Aspire is a system where AI generates, executes, and analyzes codes instead of humans writing control programs directly, thereby continuously accumulating skills. Successful control code is stored as a reusable technology library, enabling faster and more efficient adaptation to similar tasks. As a result of the performance evaluation, performance was up to 77% higher than that of the existing method, and the zero-shot generalization success rate was also significantly improved from 4% to 31%. “Separation of context and noise removal”... Nvidia unveils hybrid expansion model ‘Nemotron Two Tower’ NVIDIA unveiled the hybrid diffusion model 'Nemotron Labs Two Tower', which increases text generation speed by up to 2.42 times while maintaining the performance of existing autoregressive language models. We propose a two-tower structure consisting of two independent networks: an autoregressive context tower responsible for context understanding and a diffusion denoiser tower responsible for noise removal. Improves text generation speed by 2.42 times based on two H100 GPUs while maintaining 98.7% of existing autoregressive model performance Model weights and source code are made public through Hugging Face for use in research and industry. Google releases open source tabulation analysis AI 'TapFM' that eliminates the hassle of re-learning Google has released 'TabFM', an AI model that can perform classification and regression predictions by analyzing tabular data without separate relearning or complicated settings, as open source. By applying the in-context learning (ICL) method to tabular data, it generates classification and regression results with zero shots without the need to separately learn each new dataset. By combining the attention structure that alternately analyzes rows and columns, row compression technology, and in-context learning technology, it reduces the amount of calculation and independently identifies complex patterns between variables. We secured high generalization performance by performing pre-training only on hundreds of millions of synthetic datasets using structural causal models (SCM) instead of sensitive actual corporate data. DeepSeek doubles API fees during rush hours... \"There are limits to AI price competition\" Chinese AI startup Deepseek is introducing a surcharge system that doubles the API fee during rush hour to ensure resource efficiency distribution and service stability. Deepseek doubles the V4 model API fee between 9 AM to 12 PM and 2 PM to 6 PM Beijing time. This measure is interpreted as a signal showing the limits of the industry's game of chicken and the aggressive price cuts led by Deepseek last May. The increased rates will be applied starting with the official version of V4, which will be released in mid-July. Antropic lifts restrictions on 'Fable 5'... applies strengthened 'safety classifier' Antropic has strengthened safety measures for its AI model 'Claude Fable 5', which had been temporarily suspended due to US export controls, and resumed service. Antropic has strengthened its safety sorter and resumed its 'Claude Fable 5' service globally from July 1 following the lifting of US export restrictions. The new safety classifier blocks more than 99% of evasion attack techniques discovered by Amazon researchers and automatically switches to a lower-level model when blocked. To objectively evaluate jailbreak attacks, we are drafting a joint framework based on four factors with Amazon, Microsoft, and Google. OpenAI, ChatGPT inference cost halved... ‘Compute multiplier’ optimization strategy OpenAI is maximizing computing infrastructure efficiency by developing optimization technology that reduces inference costs by more than half. OpenAI engineers developed new inference optimization techniques to reduce model execution costs by more than 50% First applied to ChatGPT for non-logged-in users, the service was successfully operated with only hundreds of GPUs at a certain point in time. Big tech companies are focusing on the 'compute multiplier' strategy to maximize the efficiency of existing computing resources to respond to delays in securing hardware. Deepseek's first external investment of KRW 11 trillion was the shock of Antropic Mythos Chinese AI startup Deepseek was stimulated by Antropic 'Mythos' large-scale infrastructure investment and unrivaled inference performance, and began to attract its first external investment and expand computing instead of its own high-efficiency route. As Antropic's Mythos model showed overwhelming performance through massive investment in computing infrastructure, Deepseek executives acknowledged the reality that it would be difficult to survive in the frontier AI competition without a massive financial infusion. All of the secured investment will be used to expand computing infrastructure, and Deepseek plans to at least double the workforce in the AI ​​system and core development department. Despite this change in capital investment strategy, the open source-oriented AGI development route will continue, and the recently released V4 model is also significantly expanding its market share in overseas markets due to its high price competitiveness. Google Notebook LM converts documents into 60-second vertical short-form videos... Equipped with ‘Nano Banana 2 Lite’ Google has added a 'Short Form Overview' function to Notebook LM that automatically summarizes documents into a 60-second vertical short form video. AI analyzes documents, notes, and web materials uploaded by users and summarizes and creates a vertical video of approximately 60 seconds. Available first in English and available immediately on web and mobile for Google AI Ultra and Pro subscribers Produce visual materials and animations using the lightweight image generation model 'Nano Banana 2 Lite' released on the same day. [On-site] 'Garbage information' increasing rapidly in the AI ​​era... Mobizen “The solution is Ontology” Mobizen unveiled 'Graphio 2.0', a data and AI application platform based on ontology technology, to solve the problem of 'AI slop' and hallucination, which is low-quality information caused by the spread of generative AI. The rapid increase in low-quality information (AI slop) caused by generative AI raises the issue of hallucinations and the vicious cycle of AI learning. Mobizen presents ontology technology as a solution to increase the reliability of AI and minimize hallucinations by understanding the relationships and context between data. The newly released 'Grafio 2.0' is based on dynamic ontology and is equipped with hybrid RAG, etc., expanding to the stage where AI judges and acts. [ZD SW Today] Orchestra holds tech conference 'OPUS 2026', etc. This is a comprehensive article summarizing the latest business trends and event participation news from domestic software, cloud, AI, and security companies. Orchestra Group plans to hold the tech conference 'OPUS 2026' on the 28th and unveil 'Orchestro 4.0', a group-wide AI infrastructure full-stack solution. Conan Technology completed the construction of Korea Western Power's generative AI platform 'Wepibot' and held an official opening ceremony. Vibe Company's beauty specialized AI service 'Whootak' and Sage's VLM-equipped AI safety monitoring solution 'Sage Safety' will each participate in the specialized exhibition."